🐛 Describe the bug
Originally found by @drisspg:
Dynamo attempts to capture autograd.Function even when allow_in_graph or disable are applied to the function, which leads to unexpected graph breaks. For example, Dynamo introspects the following autograd.Function, even though torch._dynamo.disable is applied, which leads to an error message.
import torch
import torch._dynamo

class Func(torch.autograd.Function):
    @staticmethod
    def forward(self, x):
        print("induced_graph_break")
        return x

    @staticmethod
    def backward(self, gx):
        return gx * 0.9

@torch._dynamo.disable
def apply_func(x):
    return Func.apply(x)

@torch.compile(backend='aot_eager', fullgraph=True)
def f(x):
    return Func.apply(x)

x = torch.randn([], requires_grad=True)

f(x)

Unsupported: call_function BuiltinVariable(print) [ConstantVariable(str)] {}

We would expect Dynamo to not try to introspect the autograd.Function if allow_in_graph or disable have been applied, otherwise, the UX is confusing.
